1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,600
Every light you have ever
switched on, every phone you
have ever

2
00:00:03,610 --> 00:00:04,410
charged,

3
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:07,180
every train, fridge and hospital
machine,

4
00:00:07,190 --> 00:00:09,210
depends on one simple trick.

5
00:00:09,917 --> 00:00:12,927
It's a trick so simple you could
do it with a magnet and a coil

6
00:00:12,937 --> 00:00:13,737
of wire.

7
00:00:14,250 --> 00:00:16,170
Without it there would be no
electric light,

8
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:17,510
no internet,

9
00:00:17,770 --> 00:00:19,350
and almost none of modern
medicine.

10
00:00:20,167 --> 00:00:23,357
And it was found by a poor
bookbinder's boy who left

11
00:00:23,367 --> 00:00:26,657
school at thirteen, and never
learned advanced

12
00:00:26,667 --> 00:00:27,467
mathematics.

13
00:00:28,167 --> 00:00:29,567
His name was Michael Faraday.

14
00:00:30,107 --> 00:00:33,337
And this is how he turned
electricity from a party trick
into

15
00:00:33,347 --> 00:00:34,867
the power that runs the world.

16
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,260
People had seen electricity for
thousands of years.

17
00:00:40,083 --> 00:00:41,543
Around six hundred B.C.,

18
00:00:42,143 --> 00:00:45,253
Greek thinkers noticed that
rubbed amber could pick up
feathers

19
00:00:45,263 --> 00:00:46,323
and bits of dust.

20
00:00:47,167 --> 00:00:50,232
The Greek word for amber was
elektron.

21
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:54,000
In sixteen hundred, the English
physician William Gilbert

22
00:00:54,010 --> 00:00:56,200
borrowed it to coin a new Latin
word,

23
00:00:56,210 --> 00:00:58,157
electricus.

24
00:00:58,667 --> 00:01:02,117
In seventeen fifty two, Benjamin
Franklin flew a

25
00:01:02,127 --> 00:01:05,217
kite into a storm, and showed
that lightning was

26
00:01:05,227 --> 00:01:06,267
electricity too.

27
00:01:06,917 --> 00:01:09,477
Franklin went on to invent the
lightning rod,

28
00:01:09,857 --> 00:01:13,167
a simple metal spike that gives
lightning a safe path into

29
00:01:13,177 --> 00:01:13,977
the ground.

30
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:17,750
Then in eighteen hundred,
Alessandro Volta stacked

31
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:21,150
discs of zinc and copper,
separated by cloth

32
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:22,836
soaked in salt water.

33
00:01:23,333 --> 00:01:26,693
It was the first battery: a
steady flow of electric current.

34
00:01:27,583 --> 00:01:30,263
It was so important that the
unit of electrical pressure,

35
00:01:30,703 --> 00:01:32,523
the volt, is named after him.

36
00:01:33,167 --> 00:01:36,577
But it was weak, expensive, and
nobody really understood what

37
00:01:36,587 --> 00:01:37,387
it was.

38
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:40,570
Electricity was a curiosity for
lecture halls.

39
00:01:41,090 --> 00:01:42,710
A show, not a power source.

40
00:01:44,567 --> 00:01:47,877
Michael Faraday was born in
seventeen ninety one,

41
00:01:47,887 --> 00:01:50,227
near London, the son of a
blacksmith.

42
00:01:51,083 --> 00:01:52,223
His family was poor.

43
00:01:52,763 --> 00:01:56,003
At times he had a single loaf of
bread to last him a week.

44
00:01:56,750 --> 00:01:59,830
At fourteen, he became an
apprentice to a bookbinder.

45
00:02:00,583 --> 00:02:03,163
And instead of just binding the
books, he read them.

46
00:02:03,833 --> 00:02:06,903
His master, George Riebau,
encouraged him,

47
00:02:06,913 --> 00:02:09,833
and even let him use the shop
for experiments.

48
00:02:10,750 --> 00:02:13,900
At night he went to lectures at
a small club called the City

49
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:16,800
Philosophical Society, where he
learned chemistry,

50
00:02:16,810 --> 00:02:19,750
and how to explain science to an
audience.

51
00:02:20,667 --> 00:02:23,977
One of them was an encyclopedia,
with a long article on

52
00:02:23,987 --> 00:02:24,787
electricity.

53
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:28,180
He built his own simple
electrical machines from scraps,

54
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:29,570
in the back of the shop.

55
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:33,740
In eighteen twelve, a customer
gave him tickets to hear

56
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:37,150
the most famous chemist in
Britain, Sir Humphry Davy,

57
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:38,390
at the Royal Institution.

58
00:02:39,417 --> 00:02:40,517
Davy was a celebrity.

59
00:02:41,157 --> 00:02:44,097
Using huge batteries, he had
discovered new elements,

60
00:02:44,497 --> 00:02:46,077
including sodium and potassium.

61
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:50,720
Crowds were so big that the
street outside the Royal
Institution

62
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:53,930
is said to have become London's
first one-way street.

63
00:02:54,750 --> 00:02:57,390
Faraday took careful notes,
rewrote them neatly,

64
00:02:57,790 --> 00:02:58,970
and bound them into a book.

65
00:02:59,667 --> 00:03:02,187
Then he sent it to Davy, and
asked him for a job.

66
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,310
Davy had recently been hurt in a
laboratory explosion

67
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:09,460
that damaged his eyesight, and
for a short time,

68
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:11,460
Faraday helped him as a
secretary.

69
00:03:12,333 --> 00:03:15,583
A few months later, one of
Davy's lab assistants was

70
00:03:15,593 --> 00:03:16,973
sacked after a fight.

71
00:03:17,373 --> 00:03:19,593
And Davy hired Faraday to wash
bottles.

72
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:22,980
Faraday worked his way up.

73
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:26,890
He travelled across Europe with
Davy, sometimes treated like a

74
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:27,700
servant,

75
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:30,380
but meeting the greatest
scientists of the age.

76
00:03:31,333 --> 00:03:33,783
In Paris they met André-Marie
Ampère.

77
00:03:33,793 --> 00:03:36,833
In Italy, Faraday met Alessandro
Volta himself.

78
00:03:37,833 --> 00:03:41,023
In Florence, they used a giant
magnifying glass to

79
00:03:41,033 --> 00:03:43,793
focus sunlight on a diamond, and
burned it,

80
00:03:44,013 --> 00:03:46,093
proving that diamond is made of
carbon.

81
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,490
Then, in eighteen twenty, the
Danish scientist Hans

82
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:52,900
Christian Oersted noticed
something strange.

83
00:03:53,833 --> 00:03:57,083
A wire carrying electric current
made a nearby compass needle

84
00:03:57,093 --> 00:03:57,893
swing.

85
00:03:58,333 --> 00:04:00,393
Electricity could create
magnetism.

86
00:04:00,813 --> 00:04:02,633
The scientific world went wild.

87
00:04:03,417 --> 00:04:06,407
In eighteen twenty one, Faraday
built a small

88
00:04:06,417 --> 00:04:07,217
device:

89
00:04:07,457 --> 00:04:10,807
a wire dangling into a cup of
mercury, beside an

90
00:04:10,817 --> 00:04:11,617
upright magnet.

91
00:04:12,417 --> 00:04:15,817
When current flowed, the wire
spun round and round the magnet.

92
00:04:16,583 --> 00:04:19,263
Electricity had been turned into
continuous motion.

93
00:04:19,863 --> 00:04:22,163
It was the ancestor of every
electric motor.

94
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,250
He was so excited that he danced
around the laboratory table

95
00:04:26,260 --> 00:04:27,574
with his brother-in-law.

96
00:04:28,083 --> 00:04:30,383
That same year he married Sarah
Barnard.

97
00:04:30,763 --> 00:04:32,563
They were together for forty six
years.

98
00:04:33,333 --> 00:04:36,473
But instead of praise, Faraday
faced suspicion.

99
00:04:36,933 --> 00:04:40,363
Some, including Davy, felt he
had used another

100
00:04:40,373 --> 00:04:42,893
scientist's ideas without giving
credit.

101
00:04:43,750 --> 00:04:44,850
It hurt him deeply.

102
00:04:46,650 --> 00:04:48,690
Faraday became convinced of
something.

103
00:04:49,417 --> 00:04:52,327
If electricity could make
magnetism, then surely

104
00:04:52,337 --> 00:04:53,957
magnetism could make
electricity.

105
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:56,020
For years he tried.

106
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:57,940
He put magnets next to wires.

107
00:04:58,520 --> 00:04:59,320
Nothing.

108
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:01,170
He wound wires around magnets.

109
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:02,392
Nothing.

110
00:05:02,917 --> 00:05:06,067
Between other work, he liquefied
chlorine gas

111
00:05:06,077 --> 00:05:08,837
and discovered a new chemical
called benzene.

112
00:05:09,277 --> 00:05:11,237
But the magnet problem would not
go away.

113
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,030
In eighteen twenty four, he was
elected to the Royal

114
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:15,840
Society,

115
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:18,340
reportedly against Davy's
wishes.

116
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:22,110
A year later, he was running the
Royal Institution's

117
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:22,920
laboratory.

118
00:05:24,317 --> 00:05:27,747
Then, on the twenty ninth of
August, eighteen thirty one,

119
00:05:27,757 --> 00:05:31,247
he took an iron ring, and
wrapped two separate coils of
wire

120
00:05:31,257 --> 00:05:32,057
around it.

121
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:34,900
One coil was connected to a
battery.

122
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:38,320
The other was connected to a
needle that could detect
current.

123
00:05:39,250 --> 00:05:41,560
When he connected the battery,
the needle jumped...

124
00:05:41,570 --> 00:05:43,350
and then fell back to zero.

125
00:05:44,083 --> 00:05:46,883
When he disconnected it, the
needle jumped the other way.

126
00:05:47,667 --> 00:05:50,607
The current only appeared when
the magnetism was changing.

127
00:05:51,417 --> 00:05:54,647
Weeks later, he pushed a bar
magnet into a coil of

128
00:05:54,657 --> 00:05:55,457
wire.

129
00:05:55,477 --> 00:05:56,277
The needle moved.

130
00:05:56,917 --> 00:05:57,757
He pulled it out.

131
00:05:58,237 --> 00:05:59,617
The needle moved the other way.

132
00:06:00,333 --> 00:06:01,753
Moving magnets make electricity.

133
00:06:02,433 --> 00:06:04,633
This is called electromagnetic
induction.

134
00:06:05,667 --> 00:06:08,327
He wrote down every experiment
in numbered paragraphs.

135
00:06:09,367 --> 00:06:12,677
By the end of his life, his
notebooks held more than sixteen

136
00:06:12,687 --> 00:06:13,867
thousand entries.

137
00:06:14,667 --> 00:06:17,667
And it is the trick behind
nearly every power station on
Earth.

138
00:06:19,233 --> 00:06:22,683
Faraday then spun a copper disc
between the poles of a

139
00:06:22,693 --> 00:06:23,493
magnet.

140
00:06:23,833 --> 00:06:27,063
A steady current flowed out of
it, for as long as the disc

141
00:06:27,073 --> 00:06:27,873
kept turning.

142
00:06:28,583 --> 00:06:30,423
It was the first electric
generator.

143
00:06:30,963 --> 00:06:32,763
Turn something, and you get
electricity.

144
00:06:33,750 --> 00:06:36,220
Within a year, a French
instrument maker,

145
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:39,700
Hippolyte Pixii, built a
hand-cranked generator

146
00:06:39,710 --> 00:06:41,450
using Faraday's principle.

147
00:06:42,417 --> 00:06:45,137
It didn't matter what did the
turning: falling water,

148
00:06:45,457 --> 00:06:46,257
steam,

149
00:06:46,517 --> 00:06:47,167
wind,

150
00:06:47,177 --> 00:06:49,657
or later, the heat from nuclear
reactions.

151
00:06:50,667 --> 00:06:53,757
Today almost all the electricity
we use comes from

152
00:06:53,767 --> 00:06:56,877
Faraday's idea: magnets and
coils

153
00:06:56,887 --> 00:06:59,957
of wire, moving past each other
in giant

154
00:06:59,967 --> 00:07:00,767
generators.

155
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:04,160
Faraday couldn't do advanced
maths.

156
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:06,540
So he pictured what he couldn't
calculate.

157
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:09,810
He sprinkled iron filings around
magnets,

158
00:07:09,820 --> 00:07:12,400
and watched them snap into
curved patterns.

159
00:07:13,417 --> 00:07:15,997
He believed these lines of force
were real things,

160
00:07:16,357 --> 00:07:19,257
filling the space around every
magnet and every current.

161
00:07:20,083 --> 00:07:22,033
Most scientists thought this was
childish.

162
00:07:22,043 --> 00:07:25,533
But a young Scottish physicist,
James Clerk

163
00:07:25,543 --> 00:07:26,173
Maxwell,

164
00:07:26,183 --> 00:07:29,303
turned Faraday's pictures into
mathematics.

165
00:07:30,333 --> 00:07:32,653
His equations showed something
astonishing:

166
00:07:33,333 --> 00:07:36,303
light itself is a wave of
electricity and

167
00:07:36,313 --> 00:07:37,113
magnetism.

168
00:07:37,667 --> 00:07:41,157
Faraday had already shown, in
eighteen forty five,

169
00:07:41,167 --> 00:07:43,467
that a magnet could twist a beam
of light.

170
00:07:43,947 --> 00:07:46,587
It was a clue that light and
magnetism were connected.

171
00:07:47,333 --> 00:07:50,743
Radio, television, Wi-Fi and
mobile phones all

172
00:07:50,753 --> 00:07:51,933
grew from that insight.

173
00:07:53,650 --> 00:07:55,130
Faraday became famous.

174
00:07:55,630 --> 00:07:58,840
He also found that a closed
metal cage shields whatever is

175
00:07:58,850 --> 00:08:00,390
inside from electric charge.

176
00:08:01,250 --> 00:08:03,530
Which is why we still call it a
Faraday cage.

177
00:08:04,130 --> 00:08:07,140
It's why you're safe inside a
car or a plane in a

178
00:08:07,150 --> 00:08:07,950
thunderstorm.

179
00:08:08,417 --> 00:08:11,787
He started the Royal
Institution's Christmas Lectures
for young

180
00:08:11,797 --> 00:08:12,597
people,

181
00:08:12,717 --> 00:08:13,697
which still run today.

182
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:15,860
But he turned down a knighthood.

183
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:19,290
And twice, he refused to become
President of the Royal

184
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:20,100
Society.

185
00:08:20,833 --> 00:08:23,323
He said he wanted to remain
plain Michael Faraday,

186
00:08:23,333 --> 00:08:24,133
to the last.

187
00:08:24,833 --> 00:08:28,203
He was deeply religious, and in
the eighteen fifties he refused

188
00:08:28,213 --> 00:08:31,123
to help the British government
develop poison gas for the

189
00:08:31,133 --> 00:08:31,933
Crimean War.

190
00:08:33,483 --> 00:08:36,281
Faraday died in eighteen sixty
seven.

191
00:08:36,750 --> 00:08:39,900
A few years later, Thomas Edison
and others

192
00:08:39,910 --> 00:08:43,280
used generators built on his
principle to light up whole

193
00:08:43,290 --> 00:08:44,090
streets.

194
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:47,300
The unit of electrical
capacitance, the farad,

195
00:08:47,580 --> 00:08:48,820
is named in his honour.

196
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:52,570
From nineteen ninety one to two
thousand and one,

197
00:08:52,580 --> 00:08:55,040
his portrait was on the British
twenty pound note.

198
00:08:55,750 --> 00:08:59,220
And Albert Einstein kept a
picture of Faraday on his study

199
00:08:59,230 --> 00:08:59,640
wall,

200
00:08:59,650 --> 00:09:02,740
alongside Isaac Newton and James
Clerk

201
00:09:02,750 --> 00:09:03,550
Maxwell.

202
00:09:05,233 --> 00:09:06,853
He didn't discover electricity.

203
00:09:07,473 --> 00:09:08,273
Nobody did.

204
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,020
But Michael Faraday discovered
how to make it,

205
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:13,280
how to move it, and how to think
about it.

206
00:09:13,917 --> 00:09:17,407
So the next time you flip a
switch, remember the
bookbinder's

207
00:09:17,417 --> 00:09:17,607
boy,

208
00:09:17,617 --> 00:09:20,077
with a magnet and a coil of
wire.
